;; talk/operator--structure.scm — structure lens on surfaces/operator.scm.

(objection 'o-operator-structure-1 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'operator-needs-remote-hub :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/orchestrator/hub-node.ts" "The need says the operator sends instructions to a hub that signs with its own key, but d-operator-sign-ask is about asking the operator before the hub signs, and seven flows list transactions as signed by the operator. Whose key signs a term change or an exit, what the operator's proof of identity lets the hub do, and what the hub does if the operator is unreachable at a deadline are three real choices, and none is a decision. They sit inside one sentence of a need. Everything the person is promised about being asked rests on that unmade choice: if the hub signs on its own authority, the question is a server rule the wallet cannot enforce, not a question before a signature."))

(objection 'o-operator-structure-2 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'operator-credit-flow :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The flow signs lend-approve, which approves one borrower by name. d-operator-launch-flows leans to terms-here, whose own text says approvals come from a policy or service, and gives as its reason that approving each borrower by hand does not survive volume. The flow is the option the decision rejected, so either the leaning is not followed or the flow is wrong. No policy or service that approves borrowers exists in the kernel or the apps, so under the leaning nobody approves a borrower at all and the surface has no flow for lending credit decisions. The cost is an operator who is shown a per-borrower approval on the surface the design says they cannot afford."))

(objection 'o-operator-structure-3 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-operator-auto-credit :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/account/credit-request.ts" "The cap comes from a function of the token in code defaults and nothing the operator signs changes it; protocol.scm lists no transaction that sets it. The leaning, operator-cap, therefore has no flow that carries it: operator-credit-flow signs grant-credit, which sets one customer's line, not a cap per token. Of the three options, ask-each is the one the person's own limits rule out (they cannot answer requests one by one), so the real choice is between keeping the code cap and a protocol change nobody has proposed, and the decision does not say so. Until it does, the default operator-default-auto-credit stays in force while the decision reads as if it were being replaced."))

(objection 'o-operator-structure-4 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'operator-leaving-flow :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The job is to know when a customer leaves and that the hub answered in time, yet the flow signs exit-finish. d-operator-disputes leans automatic and operator-default-close-cases says finished cases are closed without asking, so the flow either asks the operator to sign what the surface says the hub signs alone, or lists a signature it never collects. The surface then has two readings of who closes an exit case, and the one a person sees depends on which node they read. A person who believes they close cases will wait for a question that is never asked, and a case the hub failed to close has no flow that lets the operator do it."))

(objection 'o-operator-structure-5 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'operator-needs-me :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The screen answers what the hub could not handle, and shows five per-account figures, none of which is a list of things. The thing it answers is the need operator-needs-attention (skipped paid requests, stuck chain actions, customers leaving, silent customers, each with its cost date), and no screen shows that need. The first screen is built from protocol figures, one account at a time, while the decision it follows is about a queue across thousands of accounts. A hub with many customers has the figure on each of them, so the person is handed what d-operator-first-screen says they must not hunt for. The same holds for operator-where-money, which shows per-account figures where d-operator-who-holds chooses totals per chain and token."))

(objection 'o-operator-structure-6 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-operator-defaults :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The leaning is to show each default and its risk from the first screen on, and eleven defaults are declared, but no screen has a place to show them: screens list figures and flows, and none lists a default. The first screen shows none. The leaning is therefore keep-silent in the structure while it reads keep-shown in the decision, and the operator meets the code defaults only by reading terms they never saw. The options are also uneven: keep-silent is the failure the person already has, and ask-before-customers is refused in the same text by pointing at a setup the surface leaves out, so keep-shown is chosen against two options that were not given a fair case."))
